## About

**PiMonitor** is a lightweight PowerShell script designed for anyone who SSHs into a Raspberry Pi regularly and wants automatic recovery when the connection drops.

It is especially useful in environments where the Pi may lose power, reboot, or drop off the network unexpectedly — such as home lab setups, IoT projects, or remote Pi deployments.

---

## Features

- ✅ **Auto-reconnect SSH** — automatically reconnects when the Pi comes back online
- 🔔 **Sound alerts** — plays distinct beeps when Pi goes offline and when it comes back
- 📝 **Event logging** — saves all disconnect/reconnect events with timestamps to a `.txt` file
- 🌐 **Works on office Wi-Fi** — uses TCP port check instead of ping, so it works on networks that block ICMP packets
- 🔑 **Password & SSH key auth** — supports both authentication methods
- ❓ **Smart disconnect detection** — asks if the disconnect was intentional before deciding to wait or exit
- 🛑 **Clean exit** — stop the monitor anytime with `Ctrl+C`

---

## Requirements

- Windows 11
- PowerShell 5.1 or later (pre-installed on Windows 11)
- OpenSSH client (pre-installed on Windows 11)
- Raspberry Pi with SSH enabled and connected to the same network

---

## Installation

**1. Download the script**

Clone the repository or download `PiMonitor.ps1` directly:

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/PiMonitor.git
cd PiMonitor
```

**2. Allow PowerShell scripts to run** *(first time only)*

Open PowerShell as Administrator and run:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned
```

Type `Y` and press Enter to confirm.

**3. Unblock the downloaded file** *(first time only)*

```powershell
Unblock-File -Path .\PiMonitor.ps1
```

> This removes the Windows "downloaded from internet" block that prevents the script from running.

---

## How to Run

**Step 1** — Open PowerShell (search in Start Menu → right-click → Run as Administrator)

**Step 2** — Navigate to the folder where you saved the script:

```powershell
cd C:\Users\YourName\Downloads\PiMonitor
```

**Step 3** — Run the script:

```powershell
.\PiMonitor.ps1
```

---

## Usage

When you run the script, it will first show an info screen with description and controls. Press any key to continue.

Then it will ask for your Pi's details:

```
  Enter Pi IP address (e.g. 10.182.7.147): 192.168.1.42
  Enter Pi username   (default is 'pi'):    pi
  SSH key path        (leave blank for password auth):
```

After confirming the details, the SSH session starts automatically.

### Example Session

```
  Host     : 192.168.1.42
  User     : pi
  Auth     : Password
  Log File : C:\Users\Nishant\Downloads\PiMonitor\pi_disconnect_log.txt

[2026-03-24 10:00:01] [INFO] Monitor started. Target: pi@192.168.1.42:22
[2026-03-24 10:00:02] [OK]   Starting SSH session...
[2026-03-24 10:45:12] [WARN] SSH session ended. Duration: 00:45:10
[2026-03-24 10:45:13] [ERROR] Pi went OFFLINE (port 22 unreachable).
[2026-03-24 10:45:33] [WARN] Pi still offline... (attempt 2 / 20)
[2026-03-24 10:46:05] [OK]   Pi is back online! (attempt 6)
[2026-03-24 10:46:10] [OK]   Reconnecting SSH...
```

---

## Controls

| Action | What to do |
|---|---|
| Stop the monitor anytime | Press `Ctrl+C` in the monitor window |
| End SSH session manually | Type `exit` in the SSH shell |
| Reconnect after manual exit | Press `R` when prompted |
| Exit after manual disconnect | Press any key other than `R` |
| Intentional Pi shutdown | Press `Y` when asked — script exits cleanly |
| Unexpected Pi crash | Press `N` when asked — script waits and reconnects |

### Sound Alerts

| Event | Sound |
|---|---|
| Pi went offline | 3 descending beeps 🔴 |
| Pi came back online | 2 ascending beeps 🟢 |

---

## Log File

The log file `pi_disconnect_log.txt` is saved in the **same folder as the script** automatically.

**View it in File Explorer:** Navigate to the script folder and open `pi_disconnect_log.txt`

**View it live in PowerShell** (updates in real time while the script runs):

```powershell
Get-Content ".\pi_disconnect_log.txt" -Wait
```

**Log format:**

```
[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS] [LEVEL] Message
```

**Log levels:**

| Level | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[INFO]` | General events (monitor started/stopped) |
| `[OK]` | Positive events (Pi online, SSH connected) |
| `[WARN]` | Warnings (Pi offline, waiting to reconnect) |
| `[ERROR]` | Errors (Pi unreachable, gave up waiting) |

---

## How It Works

Instead of using `ping` (ICMP) to check if the Pi is online, PiMonitor opens a **TCP connection directly to port 22** (SSH port). This means it works reliably even on:

- Office Wi-Fi with AP Isolation enabled
- Networks that block ICMP/ping between devices
- Corporate networks with strict firewall rules

```
Windows PC  ──── TCP:22 check ────►  Raspberry Pi
             (no ping needed)
```

If the TCP check succeeds → Pi is reachable → SSH session starts.  
If the TCP check fails → Pi is offline → monitor waits and retries.

---

## Troubleshooting

**Script won't run — "not digitally signed" error**
```powershell
# Run this first:
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned

# Then unblock the file:
Unblock-File -Path .\PiMonitor.ps1
```

**`.local` hostname not resolving (e.g. `raspberrypi.local`)**

Use the Pi's IP address directly instead. Find it with:
```powershell
arp -a
```
Or check your router's connected devices list.

**Script says Pi is offline but SSH works fine**

Your network is blocking ICMP ping. This script already handles this by using TCP port checking — make sure you are using the latest version of `PiMonitor.ps1`.

**SSH not found / not recognized**

OpenSSH is built into Windows 11. If it's missing, enable it via:  
Settings → Apps → Optional Features → Add a feature → OpenSSH Client

